# Copy this file to `pipeline/.env` and fill in the keys you have:
#
#     cd pipeline && cp .env.example .env
#
# This is the template for every API key the pipeline knows. `.env` is gitignored.
# The keys belong to you, they are rate-limited per account, and they must not end up
# in a commit.
#
# THE RULE: an unfilled key is never an error. `lyrebird-pipeline sources list` shows
# which sources the current keys unlock, and `sources fetch` logs
# `skip <source>: <reason>` and exits 0 for the rest. Fill in only what you use.
#
# Nothing in `app/` reads this. The browser never talks to any of these services —
# harvesting is an offline step, so the keys are only ever seen by the Python
# pipeline. An `app/.env` would be shipped to the browser by Vite, which is exactly
# what you do not want.

# xeno-canto API v3 key. Create one at https://xeno-canto.org/account
# (v2 is retired; v3 requires a key and applies rate limits).
# Unlocks: `lyrebird-pipeline harvest`, and the xeno-canto row of `sources list`.
XC_API_KEY=paste-your-key-here

# Optional. Seconds to wait between requests to xeno-canto. The default is polite;
# lowering it risks being blocked, and a research corpus is not worth that.
# LYREBIRD_XC_PAUSE=1.0

# Kaggle credentials, for the BirdCLEF competition archives
# (`lyrebird-pipeline sources fetch --source kaggle`). Create them at
# https://www.kaggle.com/settings -> API -> "Create New Token" — that downloads a
# kaggle.json; either copy it to ~/.kaggle/kaggle.json or paste the two values here.
# Also needs the `kaggle` package: `uv sync --extra sources`.
# KAGGLE_USERNAME=
# KAGGLE_KEY=

# Hugging Face token, for BirdSet (`sources fetch --source huggingface`). Create one
# at https://huggingface.co/settings/tokens. NOT required for BirdSet itself — the
# dataset is not gated (checked 2026-07-29) — but honoured if set, and needed if a
# dataset ever goes gated. Needs `uv sync --extra sources` (huggingface_hub).
# HF_TOKEN=

# Zenodo personal access token (https://zenodo.org/account/settings/applications/).
# Optional: every record `sources fetch --source zenodo` reads is open-access; a
# token only raises the anonymous rate limits.
# ZENODO_TOKEN=

# The Internet Archive needs no key at all — `sources fetch --source
# internet-archive` (xccoverbl, warblrb10k, ff1010bird) works with this file empty.

# GBIF account (https://www.gbif.org/user/profile → the account's own credentials).
# NOT required for `sources fetch --source gbif` — the occurrence-search API it uses
# is keyless — but needed if the DwC-A bulk download API is ever wired (it requires
# auth), and harmless to have ready.
# GBIF_USER=
# GBIF_PASSWORD=

# iNatSounds needs no key either — `sources fetch --source inat-sounds` streams the
# AWS Open Data archives (81 GB train split) with this file empty.
